WEBVTT


00:00:00.850 --> 00:00:04.090
Here's a no-bulk seam that
can be used anytime you're

00:00:04.090 --> 00:00:06.100
joining stitches to stitches.

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:11.280
It was suggested by fellow knitter,
Maryanne, who was knitting for a child,

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:14.820
and she didn't want any bulk at the
shoulder of the sweater she was knitting.

00:00:16.219 --> 00:00:19.700
Knit the last row of your
knitting at a very loose tension.

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:24.060
In this example, I knit the
swatch at tension 5and I  knit

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:26.210
the last row at tension 10.

00:00:26.835 --> 00:00:28.275
That isn't always possible.

00:00:28.275 --> 00:00:34.455
So try taking the tension off your working
yarn and knitting as loosely as possible.

00:00:35.205 --> 00:00:40.665
If your garment tension is at the high
end of your tension dial, hand knit this

00:00:40.665 --> 00:00:44.175
last row, loosely, not sloppy but loose.

00:00:44.235 --> 00:00:48.525
You'll see why in a minute, then
scrap off with a contrasting color.

00:00:49.035 --> 00:00:51.705
To make things easier
and set the stitches,

00:00:51.885 --> 00:00:53.745
go ahead and steam your knitting.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:58.800
I chose to work from the purl
side so I folded that waste yarn

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:03.990
under, so the purl bumps of the
last row on each edge are clear.

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:06.900
Keep the waste yarn in place for now.

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:11.760
Starting at whichever side
is comfortable for you.

00:01:11.789 --> 00:01:16.470
Use your latch tool, pick up one
loop from the front piece and

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:18.430
put that loop behind the latch.

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:24.180
Pick up the first loop of the back piece
and leave it in the hook of your latch

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:30.780
tool and pull it through, put that loop
behind the latch and pick up a new loop

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:33.780
from the front piece and pull it through.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:44.600
Strand behind the latch, pick up a loop
from the back piece and pull it through.

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:07.360
In other words, you're alternating,
pulling loop through loop, back

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:12.640
to front, front, to back working
every stitch across the row.

00:02:29.185 --> 00:02:32.245
When you're all the way across
the knitting, use the tail

00:02:32.245 --> 00:02:33.595
to secure that last loop.

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:49.549
This produces a no-bump seam and a
decorative chain joining the seam.

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:53.130
I hope you'll give this one a try.

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:58.230
Not only does it reduce the bulk, but
it provides a more stable seam than

00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:00.480
joining by weaving with Kitchener stitch.

